course: geography
lesson: 11
title: East, South and South-East Asia: countries and capitals
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: More than half of all the people on Earth live in Asia, and most of them live in its east, south and south-east: in China, India, Japan, Indonesia and their neighbours.
explain: Each country has a capital, the city where its government works, and it is often not the biggest or most famous city: Sri Lanka's official capital is Kotte, not Colombo, and much of Malaysia's government works in Putrajaya.
explain: Borders here have long, sometimes painful stories: British India was split in 1947, Korea has been split since 1945, and Thailand stayed free of European rule while its neighbours were colonised.
explain: If you played Europa Universalis 5, you saw a different map in 1337: the Mongol Yuan dynasty ruled China from Dadu, the city that became Beijing, and the Delhi Sultanate ruled much of north India; today's countries grew out of those old states over many centuries.
explain: Picture a class atlas open at Asia: the giant shapes of China and India, the long chain of Japan's islands, and thousands of islands of Indonesia and the Philippines scattered like crumbs across the sea.
fact: China's capital is Beijing, and China has about 1.4 billion people, the second most of any country, since India overtook it around 2023.
fact: India's capital is New Delhi; India has more than 1.4 billion people in 28 states and 8 union territories, and its constitution lists 22 major languages.
fact: Japan's capital is Tokyo, and its four main islands are Honshu, Hokkaido, Kyushu and Shikoku; the Tokyo area, with about 37 million people, is one of the largest cities on Earth.
fact: South Korea's capital is Seoul and North Korea's is Pyongyang; Korea has been divided since 1945, and the Korean War of 1950 to 1953 ended only in an armistice, not a peace treaty.
fact: Indonesia's capital is Jakarta, but it is building a new capital called Nusantara on the island of Borneo, and it has more Muslims than any other country.
fact: In 1947 British India was divided into India and Pakistan, and in 1971 East Pakistan became Bangladesh; millions were forced from their homes and hundreds of thousands, perhaps far more, were killed.
fact: Pakistan's capital is now Islamabad, built in the 1960s, and Bangladesh's capital is Dhaka.
fact: Thailand, with its capital Bangkok, is the only country in South-East Asia that was never colonised by a European power: it lay between British lands to the west and French lands to the east and kept its freedom by clever diplomacy (careful talks and deals).
fact: Vietnam's capital is Hanoi and the Philippines' capital is Manila; the Philippines are named after King Philip II of Spain, and Spain ruled them from 1565 to 1898.
fact: Malaysia's capital is Kuala Lumpur, though much of its government works in Putrajaya, and Singapore is a tiny island city-state that became independent in 1965.
fact: Mongolia, with its capital Ulaanbaatar, is a huge landlocked country with only about 3.5 million people, and Ulaanbaatar is the coldest national capital on Earth on average.
fact: Nepal's capital is Kathmandu and Bhutan's is Thimphu, while Sri Lanka's official capital is Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte, next to its largest city, Colombo.
fact: In 1337, where Europa Universalis 5 begins, China was ruled by the Mongol Yuan dynasty from Dadu, later Beijing, and the Ming took over in 1368.
exercise: What is the capital of China? | Beijing | Shanghai | Hong Kong | Guangzhou
exercise: Which country has had the most people in the world since around 2023? | India | China, its neighbour | The United States | Indonesia
exercise: What is the capital of India? | New Delhi | Mumbai | Kolkata | Bangalore
exercise: How many states does India have? | 28, plus 8 union territories | 50, plus 5 territories | 12, plus 2 territories | 36 states, with no union territories at all
exercise: Which list gives Japan's four main islands? | Honshu, Hokkaido, Kyushu and Shikoku | Honshu, Taiwan, Okinawa and Hainan | Tokyo, Osaka, Kyoto and Nagoya | Honshu, Hokkaido, Kyushu and Sakhalin
exercise: Why are North and South Korea still officially at war? | The Korean War ended only in an armistice, not a peace treaty | They have never stopped shooting at each other since 1950 | The war began again in 1990 | The United Nations ordered them to stay at war until they agree on one capital
exercise: What are the capitals of South Korea and North Korea? | Seoul and Pyongyang | Busan and Pyongyang | Seoul and Beijing | Pyongyang and Tokyo
exercise: Where is Indonesia building its new capital, Nusantara? | On the island of Borneo | On the island of Java, next to Jakarta | On the little island of Bali | On the mainland of Malaysia
exercise: What happened to East Pakistan in 1971? | It became the independent country of Bangladesh | It joined India as a new state called West Bengal | It became part of Myanmar | It became a British colony again
exercise: What is the capital of Pakistan today? | Islamabad | Karachi | Lahore | Dhaka
exercise: Which South-East Asian country was never colonised by a European power? | Thailand | Vietnam | The Philippines | Malaysia
exercise: Who are the Philippines named after? | King Philip II of Spain | Philip the apostle, a saint | A Portuguese sailor called Filipe | A local chief called Pilipo
exercise: When did Singapore become an independent city-state? | In 1965 | In 1819 | In 1945 | In 1999
exercise: Which is the coldest national capital on Earth on average? | Ulaanbaatar in Mongolia | Moscow in Russia | Ottawa in Canada | Astana in Kazakhstan, on the steppe
exercise: What are the capitals of Nepal and Bhutan? | Kathmandu and Thimphu | Thimphu and Lhasa | Kathmandu and Dhaka | Pokhara and Paro in the Himalayas
exercise: What is Sri Lanka's official capital? | Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte | Colombo | Kandy | Jaffna
exercise: Who ruled China in 1337, when Europa Universalis 5 starts? | The Mongol Yuan dynasty, from Dadu | The Ming dynasty, from its capital at Nanjing | The Qing dynasty, from Beijing | The Tang dynasty, from Xi'an
test: Jack says: «China's got the most people in the world, everyone knows that.» What is right? | India overtook China around 2023, though both have about 1.4 billion people | China is still far ahead of India, by about 300 million people or more | India has twice as many people as China | The United States has the most people
test: «capital of Malaysia?? its putrajaya innit» What is right? | The capital is Kuala Lumpur, though much of the government works in Putrajaya | Putrajaya is the only capital, and Kuala Lumpur is just a small village by the river | The capital is Singapore | The capital is Jakarta
test: In a cartoon, a Japanese ninja says he lives on «the one big island of Japan». What is wrong? | Japan has four main islands and thousands of smaller ones | Japan is not an island country at all, it is joined to Korea | Japan has only two islands | Japan is part of mainland China
test: «mongolia's tiny innit, like a little country» What is true? | Mongolia is huge in area but has only about 3.5 million people | Mongolia is tiny in area and very crowded, a bit like Singapore or Malta | Mongolia is a small island in the sea | Mongolia is part of Russia
test: In Europa Universalis 5 you start in 1337 and see no country called India. Why? | Today's India was formed in 1947; then the Delhi Sultanate and other states ruled | India has always been part of China, ever since the first emperors ruled from Beijing | The game forgot to put India on the map | India was under the sea in 1337
test: «bro said the capital of the UAE- no wait, i mean Indonesia, is Bali» What is right? | Indonesia's capital is Jakarta, with Nusantara being built on Borneo | Bali is the capital of Indonesia, and Jakarta is only a big port on Java | Indonesia's capital is Manila | Indonesia has no capital
test: What would happen if North and South Korea signed a peace treaty? | The Korean War would officially end, not just stop | Korea would stay at war forever by law | Japan would take over the whole peninsula at once, as it did before | The armistice would start the war again
test: Mia asks why Thailand was never a colony while its neighbours were. Which is the best answer? | Thailand lay between British and French lands and kept its freedom by clever diplomacy | Thailand was too cold for Europeans | Thailand had no coast for ships to reach | Thailand was already part of Britain, so France could not take it
test: «shut up, the capital of Sri Lanka is Colombo, obviously» What is right? | The official capital is Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte, right next to Colombo | Colombo is the only capital and Kotte does not exist | Sri Lanka's capital is Kathmandu | Sri Lanka's capital is Delhi
test: «why's it called the Philippines lol, there's no Philip there» What is right? | Spain named the islands after King Philip II and ruled them from 1565 to 1898 | They are named after a Filipino king called Philip | The name means «many islands» in Malay | They are named after Prince Philip of Britain, who visited them on a royal tour in 1898
test: «Bangladesh and Pakistan used to be the same country, right?» What is right? | Yes, from 1947 to 1971 Bangladesh was East Pakistan | No, they were never joined | Yes, both were part of China until 1971 | No, Bangladesh was always part of Myanmar until it broke away
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the population of the People's Republic of China relative to that of the Republic of India? | China, with approximately 1.4 billion inhabitants, was overtaken by India in or about the year 2023 | China retains a population substantially exceeding that of India | India possesses a population approximately double that of China | The two states have never been compared by population, there being no census in either
exam: Identify the city in which the government of the Republic of Korea is seated. | Seoul | Pyongyang | Busan, the port city | Incheon
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the partition of British India in the year 1947. | British India was divided into India and Pakistan; East Pakistan became Bangladesh in 1971 | British India was divided into India and Sri Lanka, the latter subsequently becoming Bangladesh | British India was annexed in its entirety by China | British India remained undivided until 1971
exam: State the national capital which records the lowest average annual temperature of all national capitals. | Ulaanbaatar | Moscow | Ottawa | Helsinki
